messages:
lucy: quick thread on the regional hot-swap idea. ran into an edge where two region-specific blobs disagree on tie-break ordering. what do we prefer: deterministic global tie-break or region-local?
omar: curious what the mismatch looks like — priority vs cost?
lucy: example: eu blob prefers eu-gpu-1 (lower latency) but us blob prefers us-gpu-2 for cost. a global request routed to eu for compliance picks eu-gpu-1, but an edge case where probe signals differ flips ordering.
ben: i'd push for deterministic global tie-break based on a stable key (tenant_id + model + request_hash) so results are reproducible across regions. helps debugging and audits.
natalie: reproducibility is great but can conflict with regional compliance rules that _require_ certain routes (data residency). The policy DSL should be able to mark rules as "hard-constraint" vs "soft-preference".
lucy: agree. propose: evaluate hard-constraints first (region/tag restrictions). only after those pass run comparator which uses (1) rule weight normalized by tenant caps, (2) dynamic penalties (load/cost), (3) stable tie-break key.
omar: quick note on normalization: tenants often set crazy weights (1e6). we should clamp and renormalize per-blob to a sane range and include a `weight_norm_version` in header so sim can reason about changes.
ben: +1. Also suggest we include a small signing digest in each blob: `blob_id` and `parent_blob_id`. That helps tracing which blob caused a route change.
natalie: rollout playbook thought: when we push region blobs, do staged canaries by region rather than by percentage. Regional rollouts avoid global inconsistency windows. But for global policies we do percentage canary.
lucy: ops-bot: note that cross-region rollout increases windows where different tie-breakers exist. Add a checksum harmony check in deploy-bot that warns if conflicting deterministic rules are present.
ops-bot: deploy-bot: Harmony check added: will emit WARNING if tie-break ordering differs across targeted regions.
omar: testing: add unit tests that assert tie-break stability across epoch bumps. e.g., same key, newer epoch with identical semantics should not change selection. If it does, flag as breaking.
ben: telemetry: when a route differs from preflight expectation, log `expected_blob_id`, `actual_blob_id`, `decision_path` (comma list), `latency_pred_delta_ms`. keep it small.
natalie: policy DSL proto sketch:
```
message Rule {
  string id = 1;
  oneof constraint {
    HardConstraint hard = 10;
    SoftPreference soft = 11;
  }
  double weight = 2;
}
message BlobHeader {
  uint64 epoch = 1;
  string blob_id = 2;
  string parent_blob_id = 3;
  uint32 weight_norm_version = 4;
}
```
lucy: last practical q: how do runtime evaluators handle mixed blobs mid-rollout? i propose simple fallback: evaluator selects local region blob, reads header; if header has `compat_flag: requires_central_tie_breaker` then call safe-eval RPC. Otherwise run local deterministic comparator. Keep RPC path p95 < 15ms.
omar: can we make the RPC only for the small subset of requests that hit ambiguous comparator cases? instrument comparator to early-return when difference is clear.
ben: yes. Keep RPC fallback for <0.5% of traffic normally.
natalie: owners: lucy will draft the DSL proto and deploy-bot harmony check. omar will add weight normalization and unit tests. ben will own blob tracing + telemetry. I'll draft the rollout by-region playbook and compat flags.
lucy: thanks folks, i'll open PR with proto and a short sim run. sharing initial sim summary tomorrow. :party_parrot:
